\chapter{Numerical Methods and Solver Design}
\label{chap:numerical-methods}

\section{Introduction}
The sovereign physics engine requires numerical methods capable of resolving
mixed-sign mass interactions, dynamic curvature fields, and topological
transitions. This chapter describes the integration schemes, precision modes,
and stability mechanisms used throughout the simulation.

\section{Time Integration Schemes}
The engine supports three integration modes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Euler Forward}: fast but low precision.
    \item \textbf{RK2 Midpoint}: balanced accuracy and performance.
    \item \textbf{RK4 Classical}: high precision for curvature-sensitive regions.
\end{itemize}

The RK4 update for position $\vb{x}$ and velocity $\vb{v}$ is:
\begin{equation}
    \vb{x}(t+\Delta t)
    = \vb{x}(t)
    + \frac{1}{6}(k_1 + 2k_2 + 2k_3 + k_4),
\end{equation}
with $k_i$ computed from acceleration fields.

\section{Precision Modes}
Three precision tiers are available:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{FP32}: general particle dynamics.
    \item \textbf{FP64}: metric tensor evolution.
    \item \textbf{FP128}: wormhole throat stability sweeps.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reciprocal Mass Caching}
To prevent division-by-zero anomalies:
\begin{equation}
    m_i^{-1} = 
    \begin{cases}
        1/m_i, & |m_i| > \epsilon, \\
        1/\epsilon, & \text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
with $\epsilon = 10^{-12}$.

\section{Parallel Compute Pipeline}
The solver executes on:
\begin{itemize}
    \item GPU compute shaders,
    \item multi-core CPU threads,
    \item optional distributed nodes.
\end{itemize}

Each frame resolves:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item particle forces,
    \item inertial responses,
    \item curvature updates,
    \item topology checks,
    \item anomaly detection.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
These numerical methods ensure stability across exotic-matter regimes,
wormhole geometries, and warp metrics.